diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk report claims pc1 (195.11.14.2) cannot reach pc2 (200.1.1.2), suggesting a routing table or prefix-advertisement fault. A full four-phase diagnostic sweep found no evidence supporting this claim in the current network state.

Phase 1 reachability: pc1 to pc2 completed with 0% packet loss (rtt_avg 2.33 ms) and pc2 to pc1 with 0% loss (rtt_avg 2.49 ms) — the described outage is not present.

Phase A (L2/infrastructure): infra_sweep found no ACL/firewall drop rules, no static ARP anomalies, no fabricated MACs, and all four devices are fully addressed with working default routes. l2_snapshot flagged only kernel tunnel interfaces (tunl0, gre0, sit0, etc.) carrying all-zero MACs while operstate=DOWN — this is normal Linux behavior for unused virtual interfaces, not a MAC address conflict.

Phase B (control plane): bgp_snapshot shows both FRR daemons (watchfrr, zebra, bgpd) healthy on router1 and router2. router1 (AS 1) advertises 195.11.14.0/24 and holds one Established BGP session; router2 (AS 2) advertises 200.1.1.0/24 with one Established session. No null routes, no blackholes, no command errors. tc_snapshot shows clean stock pfifo_fast qdiscs on all six inspected interfaces — no shaping or corruption injected.

Phase D (service/resource): pressure_sweep found no stress tools, CPU spikes, socket exhaustion, or daemon anomalies on any device.

Root cause: No fault exists. The network is operating correctly; the partner prefix 200.1.1.0/24 is properly advertised and reachable from pc1.

Fix: No corrective action required. If the user experienced a genuine outage, it was a transient event that has since self-resolved; no mechanism for recurrence is present in the current state.
=== END ===